<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yancai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alanliao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yancai was a nomadic people recorded in Chinese sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, living north-west of Kangju towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> and the Caspian. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Book of the Later Han</a> states that the country was later renamed Alanliao, and many scholars link the Yancai with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources and with the rise of the Alans, although these identifications are debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yancai.jpg" alt="Yancai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chinese name Yancai is usually rendered as Vast Steppe. Its people are generally considered Iranian, of the Sarmatian group. They first appear in chapter 123 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, which draws on the travels of the 2nd-century BCE diplomat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a>. It places Yancai some 2,000 li, about 832 km, north-west of Kangju, beside a great shoreless lake, and credits the country with more than 100,000 archer warriors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Chinese sources Yancai lay on the Northern Route of the Silk Route, and the Chinese sent embassies there and promoted trade. Its people are often linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aorsi">Aorsi</a> of Roman records, who held the lands between the Don and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> and were both a wealthy mercantile people and a strong military power. The historian Y. A. Zadneprovskiy dates Yancai's subjection to Kangju to the 1st century BCE; the westward expansion of Kangju drove many Sarmatians west and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a>, the chronicle of the Later Han completed in the 5th century, reports that Yancai had changed its name to Alanliao and was a dependency of Kangju. The 3rd-century Weilüe, the last source to mention it, says that Yancai was also called Alan, bordered Roman territory to the west and Kangju to the south-east, and was no longer Kangju's vassal. In the 1st and 2nd centuries CE the Alans became the leading people among the Sarmatians, moved west into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and often raided the Parthian and Roman empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chinese writers describe the people of Yancai as nomads whose customs and dress resembled those of Kangju. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a> says the climate was mild and that wax trees, pines and aconite, called white grass, were plentiful. The Weilüe adds that they raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, moved about in search of water and fodder, lived close to a large shoreless lake and were known for their sables.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yancai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese sources of the Han period refer to a people called <b>Yancai</b>, later renamed Alanliao, north-west of the Caspian, which many scholars connect with the rise of the Alans, although the identification is debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
